1}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.37, "baseline_margin": 19.52, "analyst_adjustment": 1.48, "key_factors": ["California's 12-point Democratic lean in an open-seat race", "Consistent late-October polling showing Becerra ahead by roughly 20-27 points", "All eight expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe D", "Prediction markets price Becerra at 97-99%"], "rationale": "Polls from mid-October onward uniformly show Becerra leading by 20 points or more, including high-quality surveys from Berkeley IGS, PPIC and Emerson, and every major rater places the race in the Safe/Solid D column. Markets at 97-99% and the state's strong partisan lean leave little room for doubt despite Hilton's fundraising edge in cash on hand.", "watch": "Turnout composition in a low-salience open-seat election could compress the margin somewhat, though not enough to threaten the outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Gavin Newsom (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's open governor's race pits Democrat Xavier Becerra against Republican Steve Hilton to succeed term-limited Gavin Newsom. The state's 12-point Democratic lean gives Becerra a strong structural advantage, and recent polling reinforces it: surveys from Berkeley IGS, PPIC, Emerson and SurveyUSA in late October all show him ahead by 20 points or more, with even Republican-sponsored polls showing double-digit leads.\n\nEvery major expert rating, from Cook to Sabato to Silver Bulletin, places the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets price Becerra's chances at 97% or higher. Hilton has slightly more cash on hand, but money has not translated into a competitive polling position. The race is a clear Democratic favorite, with the main question being the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 17.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0